app: hands.app runs hands and holds its macOS grants (hands-monetization-76j.3aj.8k1) (#270)

* app: hands.app runs hands run as its child and holds hands' Microphone and Input Monitoring grants itself (hands-monetization-76j.3aj.8k1)

A Swift launcher runs `hands run` from the user's login shell, so the daemon gets the
same PATH a terminal gives it, and macOS charges every grant hands asks for to
hands.app. The app quits the daemon with SIGTERM when it is quit, ends when the
daemon ends, and shows the daemon's last lines when it ends in failure. Its own
lines and the daemon's go to ~/Library/Logs/hands/hands.log.

`make app` builds it signed with the Developer ID Application identity under the
hardened runtime; `make notarized-app` notarizes, staples, and checks Gatekeeper.
`make check` type-checks the launcher.

* app: the login shell only says the environment; hands is the app's direct child, and a quit or SIGTERM always winds it down (hands-monetization-76j.3aj.8k1)

An interactive shell ignores SIGTERM while it reads its rc files, so a quit sent to a shell that would later exec
hands was lost. The shell now prints its environment (NUL-marked past rc output) and hands runs on it via env.
SIGTERM quits the app as Quit does, through a no-op handler rather than SIG_IGN, which hands would inherit. Main-thread
hops go through the run loop in common modes, so a quit's wait and an alert cannot starve them. The log is O_APPEND and
begun again past 10 MB. Bundle versions are release numbers. Check and build compile for Info.plist's minimum macOS.
Grant messages name the app hands runs in. tests/test_app.py runs the compiled launcher.

* app: env writes the environment to its own file; hands check takes the grant from a running hands (hands-monetization-76j.3aj.8k1)

A background job an rc file starts kept the shell's stdout pipe open, so the app waited forever, silently, before
starting hands. env now writes to a file only it holds, and the shell's output goes to the log.

hands check in a terminal could not see hands.app's Input Monitoring grant. A running hands starts only with the
grant of the app it runs in, so a running hands is the grant step's answer.

* app: a test's shell and log come from the environment; a second quit kills a hands that will not stop (hands-monetization-76j.3aj.8k1)

The launcher read its test overrides from argv, where anything macOS or `open --args` passes would replace the user's
shell; they are HANDS_APP_SHELL and HANDS_APP_LOG now. A second quit, or SIGTERM, kills a hands that ignores the
first: a second NSApp.terminate while a reply is pending ends the app at once and orphaned it. The shell and hands
start in the user's home with LANG set, as Terminal gives them. The environment file is removed on every path. A
failure reads only the log's last 64 kB. notarytool's own error is shown when a submission fails. hands check claims
of a running hands only that it started with its grant, and offers hands.app where this app has none.

